      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "In survival analysis, a patient who is missing complete follow-up is included in the analysis as a censored observation. The analysis makes the assumption that the censoring is noninformative; that is, that a censored patient has the same risk of death as those who have complete follow-up. We tested this assumption in a large, long-term follow-up study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "From 1986 through 2003, 14,495 patients underwent isolated coronary artery bypass grafting procedures. Of 13,963 eligible patients, 2312 were lost to follow-up. We obtained National Death Index data to complete our follow-up, and then compared survival between the original data and the complete National Death Index-augmented data."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The National Death Index data revealed 855 additional deaths and increased the total follow-up years from 86,810 to 102,157. Survival estimates and regression models did not differ between the original and National Death Index-augmented data."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Patients lost to follow-up might not differ with regard to survival from those with complete data. The requirement for 95% completeness is somewhat arbitrary. The quality and type of follow-up is more important than the percentage in time-related analyses."
        }
      ]},
